{"candidates":[{"content":"The claim in SRC_004 regarding 'lapse errors' vs 'knowledge errors' integrates with SRC_001's 'Error Cancellation Mechanism' by defining the specific failure modes that the mechanism actually filters: the panel's redundancy reduces the variance of lapse errors (stochastic omissions) but cannot filter knowledge errors, which are systemic. This relation is refuted if the panel's reliability gain is found to be independent of the ratio between lapse and knowledge errors.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"0a07b5048a67c4213e4e113cc978ea24c78a2e1e847b7b43e5b18a021b1dc074"},{"role":"dependence","target":"099a3bb5ce1903af7536de18a8c4c6a58bb2fef89caec4e8e0119db7fa6b9cf8"}],"typicality":0.9},{"content":"The proposition in SRC_003—that experts detect 'gestalt' anomalies—contradicts the premise of SRC_001 that expert intuition is merely a high-variance process subject to cognitive biases. While SRC_001 views intuition as a liability to be filtered by a checklist, SRC_003 views it as a superior high-dimensional synthesis tool. This relation is refuted if it is proven that 'gestalt' anomalies can be perfectly decomposed into a linear checklist without loss of detection accuracy.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"0a07b5048a67c4213e4e113cc978ea24c78a2e1e847b7b43e5b18a021b1dc074"},{"role":"dependence","target":"931f8d687b0a0429f9fbecbab00212877d5e59e0651c30c85b2ad88976193bfa"}],"typicality":0.7},{"content":"The 'weighting' mechanism proposed in SRC_005 abstracts the 'conceptual mistakes' mentioned in SRC_002; specifically, a 'conceptual mistake' is defined here as the failure to correctly weight variables during synthesis. Therefore, SRC_005 provides the formal mechanism for why the 'conceptual framework' mentioned in SRC_002 is necessary. This relation is refuted if the 'conceptual mistakes' in SRC_002 are found to be binary misinterpretations rather than weighting errors.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"5d003bb87b08cf8a385a535ef9316fb8941d8abbafdcf3dedf14bb18a2cc8357"},{"role":"dependence","target":"d36c185071e00b2be48ab692c428ef9c19852ca47861933528f84363962dcf9f"}],"typicality":0.6},{"content":"The claim in SRC_004 depends on the 'Type I/Type II error' framework established in SRC_002, where 'lapse errors' are mapped to Type II errors (omissions) and 'knowledge errors' are mapped to Type I errors (misinterpretations). This relation is refuted if 'lapse errors' are found to produce Type I errors (e.g., forgetting to check a 'NOT' condition, leading to a false positive).","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"5d003bb87b08cf8a385a535ef9316fb8941d8abbafdcf3dedf14bb18a2cc8357"},{"role":"dependence","target":"099a3bb5ce1903af7536de18a8c4c6a58bb2fef89caec4e8e0119db7fa6b9cf8"}],"typicality":0.8},{"content":"The reliability of the non-expert panel in SRC_001 reduces to the condition in SRC_005 that the task is 'truly algorithmic' (requiring zero weighting). If the task requires weighting, the 'Error Cancellation Mechanism' of SRC_001 is bypassed because the binary checks cannot capture the necessary pivot variable. This relation is refuted if a binary checklist can be constructed that effectively simulates variable weighting through a sufficiently large set of conditional branches.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"0a07b5048a67c4213e4e113cc978ea24c78a2e1e847b7b43e5b18a021b1dc074"},{"role":"dependence","target":"d36c185071e00b2be48ab692c428ef9c19852ca47861933528f84363962dcf9f"}],"typicality":0.4},{"content":"The 'systemic blind spots' identified in SRC_004 are compatible with the 'blind spots shared by all non-experts' mentioned as a countercondition in SRC_001. Together, they suggest that the panel's reliability is capped not by individual variance, but by the intersection of the non-experts' shared conceptual limitations. This relation is refuted if it is shown that non-experts' blind spots are uncorrelated and thus can be filtered out by a sufficiently large panel.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"0a07b5048a67c4213e4e113cc978ea24c78a2e1e847b7b43e5b18a021b1dc074"},{"role":"dependence","target":"099a3bb5ce1903af7536de18a8c4c6a58bb2fef89caec4e8e0119db7fa6b9cf8"}],"typicality":0.5}],"simulation_proposals":[]}